<p>Nasa has started the final countdown for Artemis II, a crewed mission set to launch from Kennedy Space Center in Florida on 2026-04-01 and send four astronauts aboard Orion on a lunar flyby. After about a day in Earth orbit, the spacecraft is expected to swing around the Moon and return to Earth without landing. Beyond the launch, Artemis II is widely seen as the crewed opening step in Nasa&#x27;s renewed lunar program. Related coverage also points to broader international participation and an active debate over how the agency should build out its longer-term lunar architecture.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/uBby7TivVWDa3_nmlJvZ34C-g6ACmOyBh_XV4xvU_-9pk3RHujw50aogGbKShdwQx_MkRYXPlefhnIux_sWOQ5yki4IoHX1qsUkPZ_XwqybLKDRoFi0v0S0qQJIY3SvixZUZjhBNXzd7EeIZd72A84lSL2alPI64mG3QWLoxwNzPC08epOSYPTEmfLXeTPvxPjUcK292jr3WViFuJzhadMKcWAVz-K99Mem6B65DMBXOwcRRPoWgJTwnEKeBTi_C-49nmKOGKsfLpd6MKg' alt='NASA astronauts Reid Wiseman, Victor Glover, Christina Koch and CSA astronaut Jeremy Hansen visit the Artemis II Space Launch System rocket and Orion spacecraft at Kennedy Space Center on March 30, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Crew lineup: Reid Wiseman will command Artemis II, Victor Glover will serve as pilot, Christina Koch will be mission specialist, and Jeremy Hansen will represent the Canadian Space Agency.</li><li>Flight plan: Managers said the mission should last nearly 10 days and end with a Pacific splashdown after a fast lunar flyby.</li><li>Rocket check: Nasa managers said the 32-storey Space Launch System rocket was performing well after the latest round of repairs as the countdown began.</li><li>Launch window: MercoPress reported that liftoff is targeted for 6:24 p.m. local time on Wednesday, April 1.</li><li>Moon-base sequence: In AllSides&#x27; outline of the concept, early robotics and power systems would give way to semi-habitable modules, surface vehicles, and regular cargo deliveries before permanent infrastructure is attempted.</li></ul><h3>